# Grade 11 (part c): teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'11-6-1': dict(
  saint="The Maccabean mother and her seven sons: a family who refused to eat forbidden food and died rather than betray God.",
  tryit=["Name the forms of idolatry today.", "Find the root of each.", "Compose a prayer of adoration."],
  saintStory="In the second century before Christ, the Syrian king Antiochus IV Epiphanes tried to force the Jews to abandon their faith. He desecrated the Temple, banned circumcision and Sabbath, and ordered everyone to eat pork, which was forbidden by the Law. 2 Maccabees 7 tells of a mother and her seven sons, arrested and tortured.\nOne after the other, the sons refused. “We are ready to die rather than transgress the laws of our fathers.” The first had his tongue cut out; the second was scalped; each was killed with torment as the king looked on. Yet each spoke of the resurrection: “The King of the universe will raise us up to an everlasting renewal of life.” The mother encouraged each in their language: “I do not know how you came into being in my womb. It was not I who gave you life and breath… the Creator of the world will give life and breath back to you again.”\nThe youngest was last. The king tried to persuade him with promises of wealth. The mother urged him to accept death, “so that in God’s mercy I may get you back again with your brothers.” She died after her sons. The Church honors them as the Maccabean martyrs.",
  script=dict(
    open="Set three objects on a table: a coin, a phone, and a small cross. Say, “Which of these has the most influence on how you spend your day, honestly?” Take a show of hands and a brief reason from several.",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:2-5 and Deuteronomy 6:4-5. Say: “‘I am the Lord your God… You shall have no other gods before me.’ And the Shema: ‘Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God is one Lord; and you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your might.’ The first commandment calls us to faith, hope and charity, and to worship. The Catechism connects it to the theological virtues: to believe in God, hope in him, love him above all. And adoration: to recognize God as Creator and Savior; prayer, sacrifice, keeping promises and vows made to him.”", "Say: “Sins against the first commandment. Against faith: voluntary doubt, incredulity, heresy, apostasy, schism. Against hope: despair and presumption. Against charity: indifference, ingratitude, spiritual sloth, hatred of God. Superstition: attributing magical power to practices or objects (horoscopes, charms), or excess in devotion. Idolatry: giving divine honor to a creature: money, power, pleasure, sports, nation. Divination and magic: seeking to learn the future through occult means, tarot, mediums, or spiritism. Irreligion: tempting God, sacrilege, simony. Atheism and agnosticism: practical or theoretical. Modern idols: wealth, success, appearance, technology, political ideology, even the self.”", "Say: “Images. The Church distinguishes between idols and sacred images. Since the Incarnation, Christians may represent Christ, Mary and the saints in icons, statues and paintings, which are reverenced but not adored (Nicaea II, 787). Adoration is for God alone. The first commandment calls us to an undivided heart. Jesus: ‘No one can serve two masters… You cannot serve God and mammon.’ (Matthew 6:24) The martyrs, like the Maccabees, show what it costs. And the first commandment is good news: God alone satisfies. Our heart is made for him. A practical examination: What do I think about most? What do I fear to lose? What would I sacrifice for? What do I turn to when anxious? The answers reveal what we worship.”"],
    ask=["What does the first commandment ask? (Faith, hope, love and adoration of God alone.)", "Name two sins against it. (Idolatry, superstition, despair, presumption, atheism.)", "What is the difference between an idol and an icon? (Idols receive divine honor; icons point to Christ and saints and are venerated.)"],
    practice=["List ten modern idols and the promise each makes.", "For each, write what God offers that it can’t.", "Do a personal inventory: what do I think about most, fear losing, sacrifice for?", "Write a prayer of adoration and commitment."],
    close="Pray the Act of Faith, Hope and Love. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Fast from one idol for a day.”")),

'11-6-2': dict(
  saint="The martyrs of Abitene: forty-nine Christians who said, “We cannot live without the Lord’s Day.”",
  tryit=["Trace how the day of worship moved from Sabbath to Sunday.", "Plan a Lord’s Day.", "List reverent uses of God’s name."],
  saintStory="In AD 304, under Emperor Diocletian, Christians were forbidden on pain of death from gathering for worship or keeping the Scriptures. In Abitene, a small town in North Africa, forty-nine Christians, including priests, women and a boy, were found celebrating the Sunday Eucharist in a private house. They were arrested and taken to Carthage for trial before the proconsul.\nWhen questioned, one of them, Emeritus, in whose house they had met, answered, “Without the Lord’s Day, we cannot live.” (Sine dominico non possumus.) Another, Saturninus, said, “We celebrated the Lord’s Day, as our religion required.” They were tortured and imprisoned; many died in prison of hunger.\nThe account of their trial, preserved in Christian records, is one of the great early statements of the importance of Sunday. Their motto has been quoted by popes: for a Christian, the Sunday Mass is not an optional extra but the center of life.",
  script=dict(
    open="Put a calendar of a typical week on the board. Ask students to mark in one color the time they spend on work, school and screens, and in another any time set apart for God. Notice the picture together.",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:7-11. Say: “The second commandment: ‘You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain.’ The name stands for the person. God revealed his name to Moses. The Catechism: ‘The second commandment prescribes respect for the Lord’s name.’ (CCC 2142) Sins against it: blasphemy (words against God, Christ, Mary, saints or the Church), cursing, careless oaths, false oaths, perjury, breaking vows. Using ‘Jesus Christ’ or ‘Oh my God’ as exclamations is disrespectful. The name of Jesus is the center of prayer: ‘There is no other name under heaven by which we must be saved.’ We bless God’s name: ‘Blessed be the name of the Lord.’ We begin prayers in the name of the Trinity. Our own name given at Baptism is sacred.”", "Say: “The third commandment: ‘Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy.’ The Sabbath is the seventh day, a day of rest commemorating creation (Exodus 20) and liberation from slavery (Deuteronomy 5). The early Christians moved their worship to Sunday, the day of the Resurrection, the first day of the week, ‘the Lord’s Day’ (Revelation 1:10). Acts 20:7: ‘On the first day of the week, when we were gathered together to break bread.’ Justin Martyr (AD 155) describes the Sunday Eucharist. Sunday is the ‘eighth day’ of the new creation. The Church requires Catholics to attend Mass on Sundays and holy days of obligation and to abstain from servile work and activities that hinder the joy and rest of the Lord’s day.”", "Say: “Holy days of obligation in the U.S.: Mary, Mother of God (Jan 1), Ascension (Thursday or transferred to Sunday in most dioceses), Assumption (Aug 15), All Saints (Nov 1), Immaculate Conception (Dec 8), Christmas (Dec 25). The obligation binds under pain of grave sin unless excused by illness or serious cause. We keep Sunday by Mass, rest, family time, works of charity, and relaxation. Deliberately missing Mass without serious reason is a mortal sin because it cuts us off from the source of grace. Pope John Paul II’s Dies Domini (1998) calls Sunday ‘the day of the Lord, the day of Christ, the day of the Church, the day of humanity, the day of joy.’ The martyrs of Abitene show it’s not a mere obligation but a need.”"],
    ask=["What does the second commandment require? (Reverence for God’s name; no blasphemy or false oaths.)", "Why do Christians keep Sunday? (It’s the day of the Resurrection.)", "What is the Sunday obligation? (Attend Mass and refrain from unnecessary work.)"],
    practice=["Sort 10 uses of God’s name into reverent and irreverent.", "Plan an ideal Lord’s Day: Mass, rest, family, service, with times.", "Identify which obstacles you face (sports, work, screens) and how to handle them.", "Write a short reflection on “Without Sunday we cannot live.”"],
    close="Pray the Divine Praises: “Blessed be God. Blessed be his holy name.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Keep your next Sunday as a true Lord’s Day.”")),

'11-6-3': dict(
  saint="Elizabeth of the Trinity: a young Carmelite who found God’s dwelling within.",
  tryit=["Try three prayer methods.", "Note what happens in each.", "Pick a daily practice."],
  saintStory="Élisabeth Catez was born in 1880 in France, in a military family. She was a spirited child, with a fierce temper, but in her teens she converted her will to love of Jesus. At fourteen, she made a private vow of virginity. She was a gifted pianist and enjoyed society. She longed to enter Carmel, but her mother refused until she turned twenty-one. Meanwhile, she lived like a nun at home, praying and serving.\nIn 1901 she entered the Carmel at Dijon. She had a deep sense of the indwelling of the Trinity, taught by Paul and John: “We will come to him and make our home with him.” Her prayer, “O my God, Trinity whom I adore,” expresses her sense of living in constant awareness of God’s presence in the soul.\nShe fell ill with Addison’s disease, a painful illness, and died in 1906, at twenty-six. She suffered with joy and called herself “Laudem Gloriae,” praise of glory. She was canonized in 2016.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How would you pray if you could not use words? What if you had to pray for five minutes in silence? Would you know how?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Prayer is a response to God who first calls us. The Catechism: ‘Prayer is the raising of one’s mind and heart to God or the requesting of good things from God.’ (CCC 2559) It is a covenant relationship, a communion. Three expressions: vocal prayer, meditation, contemplation. The sources: Scripture, liturgy, theological virtues. The way: the Holy Spirit is the teacher. Jesus taught us to pray: the Our Father is the summary of the Gospel. Prayer is both a gift and a battle: we need perseverance. Prayer is a school: we learn by doing.”", "Say: “Forms. Blessing and adoration: we recognize God as God. Petition: we ask. Intercession: we ask for others. Thanksgiving. Praise. Methods. Lectio divina: read, meditate, pray, contemplate. Ignatian meditation: imagine the scene. The Jesus Prayer: ‘Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me, a sinner,’ repeated in rhythm with breathing. The Rosary: vocal prayer with meditation. Centering prayer: silent attention to God. Adoration: silent presence before the Blessed Sacrament. The Liturgy of the Hours. Journaling. Walking prayer. Some are better at different times of life; the key is to find a method that helps you stay with God.”", "Say: “Obstacles. Distractions: gently return. Dryness: continue anyway; fidelity matters more than feeling. Busy-ness: schedule a fixed time. Doubt: bring it to God. Sloth: begin small. Discouragement: remember that the desire to pray is itself a prayer. Elizabeth of the Trinity found God present within: ‘I have found my heaven on earth, since heaven is God and God is in my soul.’ We pray in hope and in the Spirit. Practical plan: a set time, a set place, a set length (begin with 10 minutes), a starting prayer (to the Holy Spirit), a Scripture passage, a time of silence, a closing prayer. Pray with others too, for the Church prays together. Prayer is the response to a love that comes first.”"],
    ask=["Name the three expressions of prayer. (Vocal, meditation, contemplation.)", "What is the Jesus Prayer? (Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me, a sinner.)", "What should we do with distractions? (Return gently.)"],
    practice=["Try lectio divina on a short Gospel passage for five minutes.", "Try the Jesus Prayer for three minutes.", "Sit in silence for two minutes.", "Write which one helped you most and make a plan for a daily ten minutes."],
    close="Pray Elizabeth’s prayer “O my God, Trinity whom I adore” (a few lines). Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray ten minutes a day this week.”")),

'11-7-1': dict(
  saint="Jérôme Lejeune: the geneticist who discovered the cause of Down syndrome and spent his life defending those with it.",
  tryit=["Make a case for the dignity of every human life.", "Anticipate three objections.", "Write your replies."],
  saintStory="Jérôme Lejeune was born in 1926 in France. He became a physician and a geneticist. In 1958-59, working with Marthe Gautier and Raymond Turpin, he discovered that Down syndrome is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21. It was the first time a disability had been linked to a chromosome. He hoped it would lead to a cure.\nInstead, as prenatal tests developed, he saw that the discovery was used to detect and abort children with the condition. He spoke out, at great professional cost. He lost research funding and honors, and received hate mail. He told the story of a young patient who asked him to find a cure, and he said that the greatest scandal was a “medicine that kills the patient to cure the disease.”\nHe testified at courts and gave lectures worldwide, calling every human being a person from conception. John Paul II made him the first president of the Pontifical Academy for Life in 1994, and he died a few weeks later. His cause for canonization is open; he is a Servant of God.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud Psalm 139:13-16. Say, “Keep your eyes closed and listen for the words that describe how you were made.” After the reading, invite students to share a phrase that stood out.",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:13 and Psalm 139. Say: “‘You shall not kill.’ The fifth commandment protects the life of the innocent. The Catechism: ‘Human life is sacred because from its beginning it involves the creative action of God and it remains forever in a special relationship with the Creator, who is its sole end.’ (CCC 2258) Only God is the Lord of life from its beginning until its end. Intentional killing of an innocent person is gravely wrong. Legitimate defense of self or others is allowed, even by lethal force if necessary, since the aim is to protect life; so is a just war under strict conditions (just cause, last resort, legitimate authority, probability of success, proportionality, right intention). The Catechism now teaches that the death penalty is inadmissible, because it is an attack on the dignity of the person (2018 revision).”", "Say: “Beginning of life. The Church holds that human life must be respected from conception. Embryo is a human life with potential, not a potential human life. Abortion, direct and intentional, is gravely wrong. IVF raises concerns: it separates procreation from the marital act, often involves creating and discarding embryos. Embryonic stem-cell research destroys embryos. Adult stem cells and ethical research are encouraged. Prenatal diagnosis is allowed if it serves the child’s good but not if it leads to elimination. Eugenics is condemned. Infertility is a real cross; the Church supports ethical methods like NaProTechnology, and the option of adoption.”", "Say: “Respect of the body. Torture, kidnapping, terrorism and mutilation are wrong. Drug abuse and excessive drinking are wrong; so are reckless driving and other risks. Medical research must respect persons. Organ donation can be an act of charity if done with consent and not hastening death. Caring for the weak is a duty. The fifth commandment extends to anger and hatred: Jesus said, ‘Everyone who is angry with his brother shall be liable to judgment.’ Scandal, leading others to sin, is also a sin against this commandment. Peace: ‘Blessed are the peacemakers.’ Positive: protect life, welcome the vulnerable, build peace. Lejeune summed it up: ‘The quality of a civilization is measured by the respect it has for its weakest members.’”"],
    ask=["What does the fifth commandment protect? (Innocent human life.)", "What are conditions for just war? (Just cause, last resort, legitimate authority, proportionality, probability of success, right intention.)", "What does the Catechism now say of the death penalty? (It is inadmissible.)"],
    practice=["Write a case for the dignity of every human life using Scripture, science and reason.", "List three objections you have heard and write replies.", "Identify one action you can take to support life: volunteering, praying, advocating.", "Write a prayer for the vulnerable."],
    close="Pray the Prayer for Life or Psalm 139. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for someone who is vulnerable.”")),

'11-7-2': dict(
  saint="Rutilio Grande: a Salvadoran Jesuit who spoke for farm workers and was shot for it.",
  tryit=["Apply the seventh and eighth commandments to a case.", "Identify the duty of restitution.", "Plan a just action."],
  saintStory="Rutilio Grande was born in 1928 in El Salvador, in a poor family. He became a Jesuit, and after ordination he served in rural parishes. In the town of Aguilares, he organized the farm workers to discuss the Bible and their lives. Most of the land was owned by a few wealthy families, and the workers earned little. Rutilio taught that they had dignity as children of God and a right to a just wage and decent conditions.\nHis preaching angered the landowners and the military government. In a famous sermon in 1977, he said that if Jesus were to come back, they would arrest and crucify him again for his message. A month later, on March 12, 1977, he was driving to a village for Mass with an elderly man and a teenage boy. Gunmen opened fire and killed all three.\nThe killing changed Archbishop Oscar Romero, who had been quiet. He insisted on a single funeral Mass for Rutilio, closing every other Mass in the country. Rutilio was beatified in 2022.",
  script=dict(
    open="Present a case: a store clerk gives you twenty dollars too much in change and you do not notice until you are home. Ask, “What do you do, and why?” Take three different answers before saying anything about right and wrong.",
    teach=["Say: “The seventh commandment, ‘You shall not steal,’ requires respect for others’ goods and the universal destination of goods. The Catechism says: ‘The seventh commandment forbids theft… that is, usurping another’s property against the reasonable will of the owner.’ (CCC 2408) It also forbids unjust wages, price-fixing, corruption, fraud, vandalism, gambling that cheats, exploitation of workers, tax evasion and sloth. Rights of private property are legitimate, but they have a social function. ‘The earth is the Lord’s.’ We are stewards. Restitution: one who has stolen must return what was taken. The principle of necessity: someone in extreme need may take what is necessary to survive.”", "Say: “Work. The Church teaches the dignity of work: work is for the person, not the person for work. Workers have rights: to a just wage, rest, safe conditions, to form unions. Employers have duties. Pope Leo XIII, Rerum Novarum; John Paul II, Laborem Exercens. The economy must serve the common good. Special concern for the poor: preferential option for the poor. Defrauding laborers is a sin that ‘cries out to heaven’ (James 5:4). Also consumerism and greed. Pope Francis: ‘This economy kills.’ But the Church does not endorse any specific system; it tests every system against human dignity.”", "Say: “The eighth commandment, ‘You shall not bear false witness,’ requires truth and honesty. Truth: a virtue of being true in deed and speech. The Catechism: ‘Lying consists in speaking a falsehood with the intention of deceiving.’ (CCC 2482) Sins include lying, perjury, rash judgment, detraction, calumny, flattery, boasting and breach of confidence. Reparation: one who has harmed another’s reputation must repair it. Professional secrecy is binding, and the seal of confession is absolute. Media: the right to information is limited by truth and charity. Beauty and art also express truth. Witness: martyrs bear witness to the truth. Rutilio Grande’s justice and truth: he named the lie of an unjust order and paid the cost. Jesus: ‘I am the way, and the truth, and the life.’”"],
    ask=["What does the seventh commandment forbid? (Theft, fraud, unjust wages, corruption, exploitation.)", "What is the universal destination of goods? (God gave the earth for all.)", "What does the eighth commandment require? (Truthfulness; reparation for harm to reputation.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cases: a store clerk gives too much change; a company underpays workers; a student spreads a rumor; a journalist publishes a half-truth.", "For each, identify the commandment, the sin, and the remedy (restitution, correction, apology).", "Choose a just cause you can support and write a plan.", "Write a personal commitment to truthfulness."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make me just and truthful.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Make a restitution or apology you owe.”")),

'11-7-3': dict(
  saint="Giuseppe Moscati: a Naples doctor who treated the poor free and left a fortune of love.",
  tryit=["Examine your desires.", "Find the root of each.", "Practice one detachment."],
  saintStory="Giuseppe Moscati was born in 1880 in Benevento, Italy. He studied medicine in Naples, and became a physician, researcher and professor. He was a renowned scientist, publishing papers and making discoveries in the chemistry of glycogen. In 1906, when Mount Vesuvius erupted, he rushed to evacuate patients from a hospital before the roof collapsed. During a cholera outbreak, and in World War I, he worked tirelessly.\nHe treated the poor without charge, and even left money under their prescriptions to help them buy medicine. Rich patients were charged, and sometimes he sent them away if they were too rude. He went to Mass every morning. He lived simply with his sister, never married, and believed medicine was a vocation to serve Christ in the sick.\nHe taught his students: “Love is the only thing that can transform man. Not science, but charity.” He died in 1927, at forty-six, after a day of seeing patients. Crowds of poor people lined the streets for his funeral. He was canonized in 1987.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Name a thing you wanted for months. When you got it, how long did it satisfy you? What does this say about wanting?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The ninth commandment: ‘You shall not covet your neighbor’s wife.’ The tenth: ‘You shall not covet your neighbor’s goods.’ They guard the heart, because sin begins within. Jesus said, ‘From within, out of the heart of man, come evil thoughts, fornication, theft, murder, adultery, coveting…’ (Mark 7:21-22) The ninth commandment concerns purity of heart: the struggle against lust; custody of the senses and the imagination; modesty; purity of intention and speech. The tenth forbids greed and envy. The Catechism says the two commandments ‘require that we strive for purity of heart and detachment.’”", "Say: “Purity of heart: ‘Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.’ It is a gift and a work. How to cultivate: prayer, sacraments, discipline of the eyes and the imagination, avoiding occasions, modesty in dress and behavior, healthy friendships. Modesty protects the intimate center of the person and respects the dignity of others. It also affects what we watch and share. Detachment from goods: poverty of spirit. Not necessarily having nothing, but freedom from clinging. Envy: sadness at another’s good. A cure is gratitude and rejoicing in others’ gifts. Greed: the desire for more. A cure is generosity, almsgiving and simplicity.”", "Say: “Desire itself is not evil. We are made for infinite goodness. The problem is disordered desire. The Church says our desires, rightly ordered, lead to God. Augustine: ‘Our heart is restless until it rests in thee.’ Aquinas: happiness consists in seeing God. We should direct our desires: to ask what I truly want, and whether this will lead there. Moscati: he had success and wealth available, but chose service. Practical steps: a regular fast; give away something you value; spend time without screens; thank God daily for what you have; avoid comparison online; give to those in need. Philippians 4:11-13: ‘I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content.’ Ask for the gift of a clean heart.”"],
    ask=["What do the ninth and tenth commandments guard? (The heart; purity and detachment.)", "What is modesty? (Protecting the intimate center of the person and respecting others.)", "What is the cure for envy? (Gratitude and rejoicing in others’ good.)"],
    practice=["List five things you strongly desire.", "For each, ask: Why do I want it? Will it satisfy? Does it lead me closer to God?", "Choose one thing to give up or give away this week.", "Write three things you are grateful for."],
    close="Pray: “Create in me a clean heart, O God.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Practice one act of detachment.”")),

'11-8-1': dict(
  saint="Giorgio La Pira: a mayor of Florence who treated housing, work and peace as sacred duties.",
  tryit=["Define the common good.", "Apply it to a school or town decision.", "List its three elements."],
  saintStory="Giorgio La Pira was born in 1904 in Sicily, to a poor family. He studied law, became a professor, and was a lay Dominican who prayed daily and went to Mass every morning. He lived simply and gave away his salary.\nHe was elected mayor of Florence in 1951. When a factory was about to close, throwing hundreds out of work, he intervened: he requisitioned buildings for the homeless, helped factories remain open, and built public housing. He took a strong stand for peace. He organized conferences of mayors from around the world and traveled to Moscow, Washington and Hanoi, advocating dialogue. He hosted gatherings of Mediterranean leaders in Florence, including Jews, Muslims and Christians.\nHis motto: politics is “the highest form of charity.” He died in 1977. His cause for canonization is open; he was declared Venerable in 2018.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write the word ‘common’ on the board and ask the class to list things in the school that belong to everyone: halls, library, rules. Ask, “What happens to a shared thing when people only think about themselves?”",
    teach=["Say: “Catholic social teaching rests on a vision of the human person in community. The Compendium of the Social Doctrine of the Church states key principles. The dignity of the human person: the foundation. The common good: ‘the sum total of social conditions which allow people, either as groups or as individuals, to reach their fulfillment more fully and more easily.’ (GS 26) It has three elements: respect for the person and basic rights; social well-being and development of the group; and peace, the stability and security of a just order. The common good is not the good of the majority, nor the sum of individual interests. It is a good that is shared by all.”", "Say: “Other principles. The universal destination of goods: God gave creation for all, so property has a social function. Subsidiarity: a higher body should not take over what a lower one can do; decisions are made at the level closest to the people affected, and the higher gives help when needed. Participation: everyone has a right and duty to take part in social life. Solidarity: ‘a firm and persevering determination to commit oneself to the common good’ (John Paul II, Sollicitudo Rei Socialis 38). Together, they form a vision between individualism, which ignores community, and collectivism, which neglects the person.”", "Say: “The State has a role: to promote the common good, defend rights, maintain order and justice. The Church teaches that political authority comes from God through the natural law and must serve the people. Citizens have duties: to pay taxes, vote, defend the country, obey just laws. We do not obey unjust laws (Acts 5:29). Catholics in politics: the Church doesn’t endorse parties but insists that Catholics form their conscience by the Gospel. Pope Benedict said that politics is ‘the primary way of charity.’ Paul VI called it ‘a demanding form of charity.’ La Pira lived this. In the U.S., the bishops’ ‘Faithful Citizenship’ sets out principles. Catholics must weigh issues, with intrinsic evils like abortion having priority along with concern for poverty, immigration, the environment and war.”"],
    ask=["What is the common good? (The social conditions that let all flourish.)", "What is subsidiarity? (Decisions are made at the level closest to the people affected.)", "What is solidarity? (Firm commitment to the good of all.)"],
    practice=["Choose a local issue: a school lunch policy, a park, a housing question.", "Analyze it with four principles: dignity, common good, subsidiarity, solidarity.", "Write what each principle would recommend.", "Write a letter to a local official or plan a service."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make us builders of the common good.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read one section of Faithful Citizenship.”")),

'11-8-2': dict(
  saint="Martin de Porres: a Peruvian brother who treated the sick of every race and class.",
  tryit=["Evaluate a charity by the works of mercy.", "Distinguish charity from justice.", "Plan an act of solidarity."],
  saintStory="Martin de Porres was born in Lima, Peru, in 1579, the son of a Spanish nobleman and a freed Black woman from Panama. His father abandoned them, and Martin grew up in poverty. In that colonial society, he faced racism. He was apprenticed to a barber-surgeon, learning how to cut hair and care for the sick.\nAt fifteen, he joined the Dominican convent in Lima as a servant. He was not allowed to be a full brother because of his race. After years of devoted service, in 1603 the Dominicans accepted him. He cared for the sick in the infirmary, including the poor, slaves and nobles alike. Stories say that he healed through prayer and kindness. He fed the poor from the convent kitchen, and begged for them. He cared for stray animals. He started an orphanage and a school.\nHe died in 1639. When the viceroy came to venerate his body, the crowds were enormous. He was canonized in 1962 and is the patron of social justice and interracial harmony.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a headline about a flood, famine or refugee crisis. Say, “You have five minutes of attention and ten dollars. What do you do?” Let small groups agree on a plan and report back.",
    teach=["Read Luke 10:25-37. Say: “A lawyer asked, ‘Who is my neighbor?’ and Jesus told the story of the Good Samaritan. The Samaritan, an outsider and a hated foreigner, stopped when the priest and the Levite passed by. He bandaged the man, took him to an inn, paid, and promised to return. Jesus asked, ‘Which of these three was neighbor?’ and said, ‘Go and do likewise.’ Solidarity means we recognize all people as neighbors, and take responsibility for them. The Catechism: ‘The principle of solidarity… is also articulated in terms of friendship or social charity.’ (CCC 1939)”", "Say: “The preferential option for the poor is the Church’s teaching that we give special attention to the needs of the poor and vulnerable. It’s not exclusive; it’s a priority. Scripture: God hears the cry of the poor (Exodus 3), the prophets denounce oppression, Jesus is born poor, announces good news to the poor (Luke 4), identifies with them (Matthew 25). The Church has always served the poor: deacons, hospitals, orphanages, soup kitchens, schools. The works of mercy summarize. John Paul II: ‘Love for the poor is incompatible with an immoderate love of riches.’ Poverty has many forms: material, spiritual, relational. The Church also teaches about justice for immigrants and refugees: the Holy Family were refugees in Egypt.”", "Say: “Charity and justice. Charity meets immediate needs: feeding the hungry. Justice addresses causes: why are people hungry? Both are needed. ‘When I give food to the poor, they call me a saint. When I ask why the poor have no food, they call me a communist.’ (Dom Hélder Câmara) Catholic organizations: Catholic Charities, St. Vincent de Paul Society, Catholic Relief Services, Caritas, Habitat-type groups, and Catholic Worker houses. Practical solidarity: volunteer, give, advocate, buy fairly, welcome the stranger, and see the person. Dorothy Day said, ‘Our problems stem from our acceptance of this filthy, rotten system.’ Martin’s approach: see Christ in each person. Pope Francis: ‘The poor are not a problem; they are a resource.’ And we are all poor before God.”"],
    ask=["What does solidarity mean? (Taking responsibility for all as neighbors.)", "What is the preferential option for the poor? (Special priority for the needs of the poor.)", "What’s the difference between charity and justice? (Charity meets needs; justice addresses causes.)"],
    practice=["Choose a Catholic charity (Catholic Charities, CRS, St. Vincent de Paul) and research what it does.", "Sort its activities into charity and justice.", "Plan an act of solidarity: a drive, a visit, a letter, or advocacy.", "Write what personal sacrifice it will take."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, let me see you in the poor.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one concrete act of service this week.”")),

'11-8-3': dict(
  saint="Cuthbert: a hermit bishop who lived among seabirds and seals on a northern island.",
  tryit=["Read Genesis 1-2 and Laudato Si’.", "List duties of stewardship.", "Plan a concrete action."],
  saintStory="Cuthbert was born around 634 in northern England. As a young shepherd, he saw a vision of angels carrying a soul to heaven, which led him to enter the monastery at Melrose. He became prior of Lindisfarne, the island monastery. He traveled to remote villages on foot or horseback, preaching and teaching the people in their own dialect.\nIn 676 he withdrew to the tiny island of Inner Farne, where he lived as a hermit. He prayed and worked, growing barley with his hands. The Venerable Bede tells that Cuthbert would stand in the sea, praying through the night, and that, afterward, sea otters came out of the water to warm and dry his feet. He protected the eider ducks on the island, which became known as “Cuddy’s ducks.” Some of the oldest wildlife protection laws in Europe are said to go back to him.\nHe was drawn out of solitude to be bishop of Lindisfarne in 685. After two years, he returned to Inner Farne and died in 687.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What is one place in nature that you love? What would you feel if it were destroyed?” Take answers. “The Church has a lot to say about caring for it.”",
    teach=["Read Genesis 1:26-31 and 2:15. Say: “God made the world and saw that it was good. He gave humans ‘dominion,’ but also placed man in the garden ‘to till it and keep it.’ Dominion is not domination but responsible stewardship. We are creatures among creatures. The Catechism: ‘The seventh commandment enjoins respect for the integrity of creation.’ (CCC 2415) Animals are entrusted to our stewardship, may be used for food and clothing and for medical research within reasonable limits, but it is contrary to human dignity to cause animals to suffer needlessly.”", "Say: “Pope Francis’s encyclical Laudato Si’ (2015), ‘On Care for Our Common Home,’ draws on St. Francis’s Canticle of the Creatures. Its main themes: the world is a gift; there is an ecological crisis that is also a human and moral crisis: climate change, water pollution, loss of biodiversity, throwaway culture; the root is a ‘technocratic paradigm’ and consumerism that ignore limits; the poor suffer most; we need ‘integral ecology’ connecting care for nature with justice for the poor; and ‘ecological conversion.’ He wrote: ‘We must hear both the cry of the earth and the cry of the poor.’ Pope Benedict called for similar attention, and John Paul II named the ‘ecological crisis’ a moral issue.”", "Say: “Responses. Personal: reduce waste, reuse, recycle, conserve energy and water, eat thoughtfully, avoid overconsumption, appreciate nature, give thanks. Communal: parish ‘Laudato Si’ circles, community gardens, advocacy for policies that protect creation and the poor. The Church doesn’t endorse particular policies, but asks for prudence and concern. There is debate about the science and policies; the Church affirms a moral duty to care for creation, and calls for dialogue based on the best science. Sabbath rest also teaches limits, a counter to endless production. The liturgy uses creation: water, oil, bread, wine, fire, incense. The Season of Creation (September 1 to October 4) is observed by many Christians. Cuthbert’s ducks: a small act can be a start. Psalm 24:1: ‘The earth is the Lord’s and the fullness thereof.’”"],
    ask=["What does stewardship mean? (Responsible care for creation entrusted to us.)", "What is integral ecology? (Linking care for creation with justice for the poor.)", "What did Francis say we must hear? (The cry of the earth and the cry of the poor.)"],
    practice=["Read three paragraphs from Laudato Si’ (e.g., 1-2, 13, 49).", "List five personal actions and three community actions for creation care.", "Choose one you will do this month and write how you will check it.", "Write a short prayer of thanks for creation."],
    close="Pray the Canticle of the Creatures (a few lines). Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Take one concrete step for creation.”")),
}
